</math> times its width. It's also given that the function <math alttext="y equals left parenthesis 15 w right parenthesis left parenthesis w right parenthesis"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>15</mn><mi>w</mi></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mi>w</mi></mfenced></math> represents this situation, where <math alttext="y"><mi>y</mi>
</math> is the area, in square feet, of the rectangle and <math alttext="y greater than 0"><mi>y</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. The area of a rectangle can be calculated by multiplying the rectangle's length by its width. Since the rectangle has a length that is <math alttext="15"><mn>15</mn>
</math> times its width, it follows that <math alttext="w"><mi>w</mi>
</math> represents the width of the rectangle, in feet, and <math alttext="15 w"><mrow>
	<mn>15</mn>
	<mi>w</mi>
</mrow>
</math> represents the length of the rectangle, in feet. Therefore, the best interpretation of <math alttext="15 w"><mrow>
	<mn>15</mn>
	<mi>w</mi>
</mrow>
</math> in this context is that it's the length of the rectangle, in feet.</p>
